    {
     "tabId": "2bdcf123-db31-5e20-9c2f-c654b3a18e47",
     "collapsed": false,
     "id": "068a18ab-9aca-5ce4-b458-17179f586c8f",
     "gridsterCoords": {
      "w": 12,
      "x": 1,
      "h": 7,
      "y": 43
     },
     "type": "TextDisplay",
     "title": "How to read this",
     "config": {
      "locationUrl": "",
      "locationFile": "",
      "editorData": "<div style=\"font-family:'Metropolis','Segoe UI',sans-serif;font-size:13px;padding:12px 14px;background:#2b3137;color:#e9edf0;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.18);border-radius:6px\">\n<h2 style=\"font-size:15px;font-weight:700;margin:2px 0 10px 0;color:#e9edf0\">DOM &mdash; how is each layer of a vSAN write performing?</h2>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\">DOM is the distributed object manager, and it has three layers. The <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">client</b> is the host a guest is running on. The <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">owner</b> is the host that coordinates a given object's writes, which may be a different host entirely. The <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">component manager</b> is what talks to the disks.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\">The same measures are reported at each layer. This screen shows the owner and the component manager at cluster level, and the client, the owner and the component manager for each host. The cluster-level client table is on the Overview and Backpressure screens.</p>\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;font-weight:700;margin:16px 0 6px 0;color:#e9edf0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.18);padding-bottom:3px\">Reading any column here</h3>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">A blank cell is not a zero.</b> A blank cell means no value was collected for that column in the selected period.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">Raw columns are cumulative counts.</b> Where a column has a Raw twin, the Raw column is the cumulative count and the other column is a per-interval rate.</p>\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;font-weight:700;margin:16px 0 6px 0;color:#e9edf0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.18);padding-bottom:3px\">Cluster - owner</h3>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\">Renders the <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">vSAN Cluster DOM Owner</b> view.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Read Lat</span> &mdash; Average read latency at this layer. For the client, component manager and zDOM layers the vSAN Performance Service graph definition marks 15 ms as warning and 30 ms as critical; it defines no threshold for the owner layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Write Lat</span> &mdash; Average write latency at this layer. For the client, component manager and zDOM layers the vSAN Performance Service graph definition marks 15 ms as warning and 30 ms as critical; it defines no threshold for the owner layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Read IOPS</span> &mdash; Read operations per second.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Write IOPS</span> &mdash; Write operations per second.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Read Throughput</span> &mdash; Bytes per second read. The owner layer names its throughput columns differently from the client layer; the quantity is the same.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Write Throughput</span> &mdash; Bytes per second written.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">OIO</span> &mdash; Outstanding IO: operations issued and not yet completed. Latency multiplied by nothing tells you why it is high -- this does. High latency with high outstanding IO is queueing, high latency with low outstanding IO is a slow device.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Congestions</span> &mdash; vSAN congestion raised at this layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Read Congestions</span> &mdash; Backpressure attributed to reads.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Write Congestions</span> &mdash; Backpressure attributed to writes.</p>\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;font-weight:700;margin:16px 0 6px 0;color:#e9edf0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.18);padding-bottom:3px\">Cluster - component manager</h3>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\">Renders the <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">vSAN Cluster DOM Component Manager</b> view.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Read Lat</span> &mdash; Average read latency at this layer. For the client, component manager and zDOM layers the vSAN Performance Service graph definition marks 15 ms as warning and 30 ms as critical; it defines no threshold for the owner layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Write Lat</span> &mdash; Average write latency at this layer. For the client, component manager and zDOM layers the vSAN Performance Service graph definition marks 15 ms as warning and 30 ms as critical; it defines no threshold for the owner layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Congestions</span> &mdash; vSAN congestion raised at this layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">OIO</span> &mdash; Outstanding IO: operations issued and not yet completed. Latency multiplied by nothing tells you why it is high -- this does. High latency with high outstanding IO is queueing, high latency with low outstanding IO is a slow device.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Read IOPS</span> &mdash; Read operations per second.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Write IOPS</span> &mdash; Write operations per second.</p>\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;font-weight:700;margin:16px 0 6px 0;color:#e9edf0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.18);padding-bottom:3px\">Per host - client</h3>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\">Renders the <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">vSAN Host DOM</b> view.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Read Lat</span> &mdash; Average read latency at this layer. For the client, component manager and zDOM layers the vSAN Performance Service graph definition marks 15 ms as warning and 30 ms as critical; it defines no threshold for the owner layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Write Lat</span> &mdash; Average write latency at this layer. For the client, component manager and zDOM layers the vSAN Performance Service graph definition marks 15 ms as warning and 30 ms as critical; it defines no threshold for the owner layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Congestions</span> &mdash; vSAN congestion raised at this layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Read IOPS</span> &mdash; Read operations per second.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Write IOPS</span> &mdash; Write operations per second.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">OIO</span> &mdash; Outstanding IO: operations issued and not yet completed. Latency multiplied by nothing tells you why it is high -- this does. High latency with high outstanding IO is queueing, high latency with low outstanding IO is a slow device.</p>\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;font-weight:700;margin:16px 0 6px 0;color:#e9edf0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.18);padding-bottom:3px\">Per host - owner</h3>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\">Renders the <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">vSAN Host DOM Owner</b> view.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Seg clean unmap congestion</span> &mdash; Congestion attributed to segment cleaner unmap work.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Resync Read Congestions</span> &mdash; Congestion reported under the id recoveryWriteCongestion. The Performance Service publishes it under the name \"Resync Read Congestions\", and the column label is that Broadcom name.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Write Lat</span> &mdash; Average write latency at this layer. For the client, component manager and zDOM layers the vSAN Performance Service graph definition marks 15 ms as warning and 30 ms as critical; it defines no threshold for the owner layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Lat (max, write)</span> &mdash; Worst write latency in the interval.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Lat stddev (write)</span> &mdash; Standard deviation of write latency.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Read Lat</span> &mdash; Average read latency at this layer. For the client, component manager and zDOM layers the vSAN Performance Service graph definition marks 15 ms as warning and 30 ms as critical; it defines no threshold for the owner layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Lat (max, read)</span> &mdash; Worst read latency in the interval.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">High outstanding IO duration % (write)</span> &mdash; High outstanding IO duration for writes, as a percentage of the interval.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Owner2 pc commit latency avg us</span> &mdash; Average latency, in microseconds, of the owner's 2PC (two-phase commit) of a write.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Write leaf owner latency local</span> &mdash; Write latency for replicas on this host.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Write leaf owner latency remote</span> &mdash; Write latency for replicas on other hosts.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Read leaf owner latency local</span> &mdash; Read latency served from this host's own disks.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Read leaf owner latency remote</span> &mdash; Read latency served from another host's disks.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Server switch count</span> &mdash; Cumulative count of server switches.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">IOPS</span> &mdash; Total operations per second at this layer, reads and writes together.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">OIO</span> &mdash; Outstanding IO: operations issued and not yet completed. Latency multiplied by nothing tells you why it is high -- this does. High latency with high outstanding IO is queueing, high latency with low outstanding IO is a slow device.</p>\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;font-weight:700;margin:16px 0 6px 0;color:#e9edf0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.18);padding-bottom:3px\">Per host - component manager</h3>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\">Renders the <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">vSAN Host DOM Component Manager</b> view.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Component congestion</span> &mdash; Congestion raised against a single component. Shown beside shared congestion on the same row.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Shared congestion</span> &mdash; Shared congestion, as distinct from congestion raised against a single component.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Resync read congestion</span> &mdash; Congestion attributed to resync reads.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Read Lat</span> &mdash; Average read latency at this layer. For the client, component manager and zDOM layers the vSAN Performance Service graph definition marks 15 ms as warning and 30 ms as critical; it defines no threshold for the owner layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Lat (max, read)</span> &mdash; Worst read latency in the interval.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Write Lat</span> &mdash; Average write latency at this layer. For the client, component manager and zDOM layers the vSAN Performance Service graph definition marks 15 ms as warning and 30 ms as critical; it defines no threshold for the owner layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Lat (max, write)</span> &mdash; Worst write latency in the interval.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Lat stddev (write)</span> &mdash; Standard deviation of write latency.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Read IOPS</span> &mdash; Read operations per second.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Write IOPS</span> &mdash; Write operations per second.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">OIO</span> &mdash; Outstanding IO: operations issued and not yet completed. Latency multiplied by nothing tells you why it is high -- this does. High latency with high outstanding IO is queueing, high latency with low outstanding IO is a slow device.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Read Throughput</span> &mdash; Bytes per second read at this layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Write Throughput</span> &mdash; Bytes per second written at this layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">VM disk queue depth (write)</span> &mdash; Queue depth for virtual disk writes at the component manager.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">VM disk dispatched cost (write)</span> &mdash; Cost of dispatched virtual disk writes, as reported by the component manager.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Namespace dispatched cost (write)</span> &mdash; Cost of dispatched writes to namespace objects, which hold VM configuration files, as reported by the component manager.</p>\n</div>\n",
